    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d473">

                <pb facs="0643" n="551" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v01/pg551"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 103.97/211a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Food Administrator</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hoover</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Page</hi>)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d473-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-05-20T19:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 20, 1918, 7 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Will you kindly inform Sheldon,
                        Durand, Simms and others
                    interested that do not believe any conclusive program of next year’s food
                    supplies to Allies can be arrived at before the middle of July when all our
                    crops are defined and our animal resources recounted. In this view I propose to
                    come myself to Europe with technical staff to consider the whole situation with
                    our European food colleagues.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>[<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Hoover</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d473-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d473-f1-content">
                <p>Transmitted by the Secretary of State, No. 7836.</p>
